-- -*- mode: lua -*-

-- Launchpad map, Albert Gräf <agggraef@gmail.com>, 2 April 2024

-- IMPORTANT: In contrast to the other controllers, this device needs to be
-- connected to its own MIDI port, port #3 or #4, on *both* input and output.

--[[

   Here are some notes about the Raptor Launchpad implementation which should
   hopefully help you get started. This assumes that you're already familiar
   with the Launchpad. If not, it's probably a good idea to skim the user
   guide for your device first.

   HARDWARE:

   You'll need a recent Launchpad version, either the Launchpad X, the
   Launchpad Mini MK3, or the Launchpad Pro MK3. All three have been tested
   and are known to work. Apart from the hardware differences, all three
   devices should work pretty much the same in Raptor. Obviously, the Mini and
   the X have less buttons, which is why we provide lots of alternative
   bindings so that all the available functionality is accessible on all
   devices. The Launchpad Mini has some additional limitations:

   - It doesn't have a real note mode with switchable drum rack like the Pro
     and the X do, thus you'll have to use its custom drum and key layouts
     instead.

   - The pads on the launch grid aren't velocity-sensitive, so there are no
     "mapping tooltips", and the "latching faders" are two-valued, either on
     (127) or off (0), and thus function more like a momentary toggle.

   MAPPING:

   The Launchpad driver makes good use of all the nifty features that the
   Launchpad has on offer:

   - A session view with the launch grid, where each pad can be mapped using
     MIDI learn and function either as a toggle or as a "latching fader",
     depending on the Raptor control it is mapped to. Also, the two bottom
     rows of the launch grid work in the same way as the two button rows of
     the Launch Control XL mapping.

   - Four fader banks which function like the Volume faders and the Pan, Send
     A, and Send B rotary encoders of the Launch Control XL. In this mode each
     bank consists of 8 vertical faders which can be operated in the same way
     as the track and mixer controls described in the Launchpad X and Pro MK3
     user guides. (The Launchpad Mini MK3 works the same, although it doesn't
     offer the velocity-sensitive fader operation of its two siblings.)

   - The hardware buttons surrounding the launch grid which are used for
     switching fader banks, selecting Raptor instances and presets, and for
     other MIDI-mapped Raptor functions, while retaining full integration with
     Launchpad's built-in functionality such as the note and drum views.

   Some of the hardware buttons work differently than what you might be used
   to from Ableton Live, however. In particular, the Mute, Solo, and Record
   Arm buttons are bound to Raptor's mute, latch, and bypass "voice" controls.

   On the Launchpad Pro, the Stop Clip button toggles between Launchpad's note
   and drum views. On the Launchpad X, you can just press the Notes button
   instead, while the Mini lets you switch between the two custom layouts by
   pressing the Keys and Drums buttons.

   In the following we discuss each of the elements of the Raptor mapping in
   more detail.

   FADER BANKS: The fader setup largely mimics the Launch Control XL map
   (which see), with the faders and knobs of the XL on the Launchpad's Volume,
   Pan, and Send A/B fader banks. The CC numbers have been chosen to be
   compatible (see the Lua table below for the actual numbers if you want to
   use these for a custom Launchpad layout).

   The fader banks 1-4 can be accessed using either the corresponding
   Volume/Pan/Sends/Device buttons in the bottom row of the Launchpad Pro MK3,
   or with the topmost four scene launch buttons in session mode, labeled
   Volume, Pan, Send A, and Send B on the Launchpad X (which are also the
   labels we use in the following, since they nicely match up with the
   labeling on the Launch Control XL). The latter binding works on all
   devices, but is only active in session mode. (Which is incovenient, but
   Novation made it so that these buttons only function in that mode.)

   A click of the button activates the corresponding fader bank. Another click
   of the same button then returns you to the most recent (non-fader) view.
   You can also press and *hold* the button to momentarily switch to the fader
   page, so that you can quickly change some faders, and revert to the
   previous view as soon as you release the button again. (Note that on the
   Launchpad X and Mini that previous view will *always* be the session view,
   because that is the only way to invoke the fader banks. On the Launchpad
   Pro, however, you can switch to the fader banks from any of the session,
   note, drum, and chord views, using the mixer buttons at the bottom.)

   The fader positions shown on the device will always reflect the current
   parameter values in the Raptor patch. If you're running multiple Raptor
   instances, that will be the values in the selected Raptor instance, or the
   values of the time master, if no Raptor instance is selected. The values
   are also updated live while switching instances, or if a value is changed
   in the patch (or via some other connected controller).

   The available fader banks are mapped as follows:

   1: Volume: 1 = pitch hi, 2 = pitch lo, 3 = pattern mode (random, up, down,
   etc.), 4 = pos/anacrusis (the "scrubbing" control), 5 = tempo, 6 = meter
   numerator, 7 = meter denominator, 8 = division. Note that these are all
   arpeggiator controls in the panel, except #4 pos/anacrusis which is in the
   time subpatch.

   2: Pan = bipolar mod controls (panel).

   3/4: Send A/B = unipolar min/max controls (panel).

   Here's the detailed map of all fader pages, with some of the labels
   abbreviated so that each bank fits on a single line:

   1  [hi*]    [lo*]  [mode] [pos*]    [tempo] [num]     [denom] [divis]
   2* [velmod] [pmod] [gain] [gatemod] [hmod]  [prefmod] [smod]  [nmod]
   3  [minvel] [pmin] [wmin] [gate]    [hmin]  [pref]    [smin]  [uniq]
   4  [maxvel] [pmax] [wmax] [gate]    [hmax]  [pref]    [smax]  [nmax]

   NOTE: The three faders on bank 1 (Volume) that are marked with an asterisk
   are bipolar, as are *all* the faders on bank 2 (Pan). The other faders are
   all unipolar.

   NB: This table and the following one are also included in the distribution
   as a nicely formatted pdf document, see doc/raptor7-cheatsheet.pdf. You
   might want to print this and stick it to your Launchpad for reference. :)

   LAUNCH GRID: Other functions are accessible using the launch grid in
   session view. The pre-assigned functions are color-coded and laid out in
   the two pad rows at the bottom, following the same layout as the two button
   rows on the Launch Control XL:

     - voice controls -                       - looper controls -
   [mute] [latch] [bypass]  [      ] [      ] [load] [save] [loop]
                  [raptor]  [rewind]          [prev] [next] [play]

   NB: The mute, latch, and bypass toggles are also accessible using hardware
   buttons, see below.

   PAD CONTROLS: The pads on the launch grid have a sensitivity threshold; a
   medium touch will trigger them, while a light touch just displays the
   current mapping and value (if any) as a "tooltip" in the console window.
   Note that this only works on the Launchpad X and Pro MK3, as the pads on
   the Launchpad Mini MK3 are not velocity-sensitive.

   INTELLIGENT MAPPING: The pads can all be mapped using MIDI learn, in which
   case they light up to indicate that they are bound to a Raptor control.
   The exact behavior of each pad depends on the type of control it is mapped
   to. For toggled controls, it acts like a toggle button. Otherwise, it
   works like a latching fader which inputs a value depending on the velocity
   (if it is above the threshold) and snaps back to 0 (or the middle position,
   in the case of a bipolar control) as soon as the pad is released.

   HARDWARE BUTTONS: The Launchpad's hardware buttons positioned around the
   launch grid will also be lit up. Some of them can also be MIDI-mapped. The
   precise set of hardware buttons that you have depends on your type of
   device, so the button assignments may vary. (However, we try to ensure that
   all available functionality is available on *all* devices in some way.)

   Here is a brief rundown of the available functions:

   The "Play" and "Capture MIDI" buttons on the Launchpad Pro, as well as the
   "Capture MIDI" button on the Launchpad X, are bound to Raptor's play and
   loop functions, respectively. If you don't have these then you can use the
   corresponding pads in the session view instead (see above).

   The left and right arrow buttons can be used to select the current Raptor
   instance, while the up and down arrow buttons change presets. Note that
   these buttons are in slightly different locations depending on the
   Launchpad model. Also note that these functions can only be accessed in the
   session view, in other modes the buttons may have a different meaning
   (i.e., transposition in note mode), or are just unavailable because
   Novation made it so.

   On the Launchpad Pro, instances can also be selected using the upper button
   row at the bottom, and the bottommost row has the following functions:

   Record Arm/Mute/Solo: These are mapped to the bypass/mute/latch toggles,
   which are also on the launch grid, albeit in a different order (see above).

   Volume/Pan/Sends/Device: Access to the four fader pages (see above).

   Stop Clip: Toggle between the standard note view and the drumrack view.

   On all Launchpad versions, the same functionality is replicated on the
   scene launch buttons in session mode, in a different order and labeled
   slightly differently, but using the same button colors: Volume, Pan, Send
   A, Send B, Stop Clip, Mute, Solo, Record Arm. (Note that these precise
   labels are only on the Launchpad X, but the buttons will work the same also
   on the Mini and the Pro.)

]]

{
  -- alternative bindings for the mute/latch/bypass controls, uses the mute,
  -- solo, and record arm hardware buttons in the bottom row (LP Pro only)
  [1] = { [33] = { "bypass", true } },
  [2] = { [33] = { "mute", true } },
  [3] = { [33] = { "latch", true } },
  -- alternative bindings for the mute/latch/bypass controls, uses the last
  -- three scene launch buttons (Mute, Solo, Record Arm on the Launchpad X);
  -- these should work on all Launchpad versions
  [39] = { [33] = { "mute", true } },
  [29] = { [33] = { "latch", true } },
  [19] = { [33] = { "bypass", true } },
  -- Play and Capture MIDI buttons (LP Pro)
  [20] = { [33] = { "play", true } },
  [10] = { [33] = { "loop", true } },
  -- Capture MIDI button (LP X)
  [98] = { [33] = { "loop", true } },
  -- send A (min value) faders
  [13] = { [37] = "minvel" },
  [14] = { [37] = "pmin" },
  [15] = { [37] = "wmin" },
  [16] = { [37] = "gate" },
  [17] = { [37] = "hmin" },
  [18] = { [37] = "pref" },
  [19] = { [37] = "smin", [33] = { "bypass", true } },
  [20] = { [37] = "uniq" },
  -- send B (max value) faders
  [29] = { [37] = "maxvel", [33] = { "latch", true } },
  [30] = { [37] = "pmax" },
  [31] = { [37] = "wmax" },
  [32] = { [37] = "gate" },
  [33] = { [37] = "hmax" },
  [34] = { [37] = "pref" },
  [35] = { [37] = "smax" },
  [36] = { [37] = "nmax" },
  -- pan (bipolar mod) faders
  [49] = { [37] = "velmod" },
  [50] = { [37] = "pmod" },
  [51] = { [37] = "gain" },
  [52] = { [37] = "gatemod" },
  [53] = { [37] = "hmod" },
  [54] = { [37] = "prefmod" },
  [55] = { [37] = "smod" },
  [56] = { [37] = "nmod" },
  -- volume faders
  [77] = { [37] = "pitchhi" },
  [78] = { [37] = "pitchlo" },
  [79] = { [37] = "mode" },
  [80] = { [37] = "pos" },
  [81] = { [37] = "tempo" },
  [82] = { [37] = "meter-num" },
  [83] = { [37] = "meter-denom" },
  [84] = { [37] = "division" },
  -- predefined pads (session view)
  -- 139, 140 unassigned
  [141] = { [33] = { "raptor", true } },
  [142] = { [33] = { "rewind", true } },
  -- 143 unassigned
  [144] = { [33] = { "loop-prev", true } },
  [145] = { [33] = { "loop-next", true } },
  [146] = { [33] = { "play", true } },
  -- upper row
  [149] = { [33] = { "mute", true } },
  [150] = { [33] = { "latch", true } },
  [151] = { [33] = { "bypass", true } },
  -- 152, 153 unassigned
  [154] = { [33] = { "loop-load", true } },
  [155] = { [33] = { "loop-save", true } },
  [156] = { [33] = { "loop", true } },
}
